# DEPENDENCY.md — Xury layer dependency rules

## Purpose

This file states which layer may include/call which other layer. It
exists because "phase number" and "safe to depend on" are not the
same thing — a module can be phase-adjacent on paper but still
create a circular or backward dependency if included carelessly.
When in doubt, check here before adding an `#include`.

The general rule: **a layer may depend on anything strictly below it
in the dependency graph, and only on foundational (non-orchestrating)
files within its own or adjacent layers.** Orchestration/wrapper
files that will eventually call *back into* the layer you're writing
must never be depended on by that layer.

---

## The dependency graph (bottom to top)

```
core/            (Phase C)  — sock dispatch, mem, time, log, endian,
                               bytes, rand — foundation, no NAT
                               knowledge at all
platform/        (Phase D)  — posix/linux real syscalls, implements
                               core/'s dispatch targets
api/ (foundational subset)  — types.c, err.c, config.c, weapon.c,
                               hooks.c, version.c — public type
                               system utilities, no NAT logic
scan/            (Phase F)  — sensing, probing, math (F.3a)
analysis/        (Phase G)  — classify (F.3b), score (F.3c)
smart/           (Phase J)  — cache, early_term, learning, adaptive
weapons/         (Phase H)  — ipv6, upnp, natpmp, pcp, hole, predict
peer/            (Phase I)  — mirror, relay, upgrade
sweet/           (Phase K)  — cgnat, exploit, mimic, negotiate, adapt
blitz/           (Phase L)  — race, winner, orchestration across weapons
engine/          (Phase M)  — context.h (cycle-breaker), lifecycle,
                               orchestrator
api/ (orchestration subset) (Phase N) — scan.c, engine.c, peer.c —
                               these WRAP the layers above; nothing
                               below may depend on them
```

---

## Rule: `api/` is two different things in one folder

This is the single most important rule to internalize, because the
folder name alone doesn't tell you which kind of file you're looking
at.

**Foundational `api/*.c`** (already built, Phase 1-ish):
`types.c`, `err.c`, `weapon.c`, `config.c`, `hooks.c`, `version.c`.
These are public type-system utilities. They contain no NAT-specific
orchestration logic and have no dependency on `scan/`, `analysis/`,
`weapons/`, or anything above. **Any layer may depend on these.**

**Orchestration-wrapper `api/*.c`** (Phase N, not yet built):
`scan.c`, `engine.c`, `peer.c`. These will *wrap* `scan/`,
`analysis/`, `engine/`, etc. as the public-facing interface.
**Nothing below Phase N may depend on these** — doing so creates an
immediate circular dependency, since these files exist specifically
to call into the lower layers.

Concretely: `scan/sensing.c` including `api/internal/types.h` is
fine (foundational). `scan/sensing.c` including `api/internal/scan.h`
would not be fine (that header belongs to the orchestration wrapper
that will one day call `sensing.c`, not the other way around).

